      "writtenOn": "2026-06-02T10:19:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m really sorry, I feel like I\u0027m wasting your time because I actually really do not know much about all of this and it feels disrespectful of me to forward you rephrased LLM answers.\n\nIf you also feel this is not getting anywhere or too frustrating for you to work with, feel free to close this.\n\nI can only provide one more thing, which is a bunch of \"given x, expect y, got z\" cases, which might help you understand what I\u0027m trying to address with the proposed fix:\n\n# Concrete Row-Major Square-Matrix Examples\n\nThese examples come from small standalone SPIR-V assembly reproducers. The\n\"got\" output is Tint before the patch; the \"expected\" output is the same inputs\nthrough Tint with this patch applied.\n\nThe common setup is a SPIR-V `mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e` member decorated with `RowMajor` and `MatrixStride 16`. Because the matrix is square, the rewritten WGSL matrix type is still `mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e`, so type comparison alone does not reveal that a semantic transpose is still required.\n\n## 1. Uniform Square Whole-Matrix Load\n\nGiven a row-major uniform `mat4x4` loaded as a whole matrix and used in `OpVectorTimesMatrix`.\n\nExpected:\n\n```wgsl\nv \u003d (vec4\u003cf32\u003e(in_pos.x, in_pos.y, in_pos.z, 1.0f) * transpose(u.m));\n```\n\nGot before the patch:\n\n```wgsl\nv \u003d (vec4\u003cf32\u003e(in_pos.x, in_pos.y, in_pos.z, 1.0f) * u.m);\n```\n\nThis is the simplest square-matrix failure: the matrix type stays `mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e`, but the logical value still needs to be transposed when read from row-major storage.\n\n## 2. Uniform Square Chained Column Load\n\nGiven a row-major uniform `mat4x4` accessed through chained access chains, equivalent to loading `u.m[1]`.\n\nExpected:\n\n```wgsl\nlet v_1 \u003d \u0026(u.m);\nv \u003d tint_load_row_major_column(v_1, u32(1i));\n```\n\nGot before the patch:\n\n```wgsl\nv \u003d u.m[1i];\n```\n\nThe direct `u.m[1i]` reads the physical column as if it were the logical column. The helper reconstructs the logical column from row-major storage.\n\n## 3. Uniform Square Chained Scalar Load\n\nGiven a scalar element load from a row-major square matrix, equivalent to `u.m[1].z`.\n\nExpected:\n\n```wgsl\nlet v_1 \u003d \u0026(u.m);\nlet v_2 \u003d (*(v_1))[2i].y;\nv \u003d vec4\u003cf32\u003e(v_2, v_2, v_2, v_2);\n```\n\nGot before the patch:\n\n```wgsl\nlet v_1 \u003d u.m[1i].z;\nv \u003d vec4\u003cf32\u003e(v_1, v_1, v_1, v_1);\n```\n\nThis shows the row/column swap needed for element access. The logical `m[1].z` maps to physical `[2].y` for row-major storage.\n\n## 4. Storage Square Whole-Matrix Store\n\nGiven a whole-matrix store to a row-major storage-buffer `mat4x4`.\n\nExpected:\n\n```wgsl\nb.m \u003d transpose(mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e(\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f)));\n```\n\nGot before the patch:\n\n```wgsl\nb.m \u003d mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e(\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f));\n```\n\nThe store direction has the symmetric issue: the logical matrix must be transposed before writing into row-major physical storage.\n\n## 5. Function Struct Construction With Row-Major Square Member\n\nGiven a function-local struct with a row-major square matrix member, constructed from a `mat4x4` and then read back.\n\nExpected:\n\n```wgsl\nv_1 \u003d S_1_1(transpose(mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e(\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f))));\nv \u003d (vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f) * transpose(v_1.m));\n```\n\nGot before the patch:\n\n```wgsl\nv_1 \u003d S_1_1(mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e(\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(3.0f, 4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(4.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f)));\nv \u003d (vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f) * v_1.m);\n```\n\nThis is why the patch consults the original struct metadata after rebuilding the struct. The rewritten struct member no longer carries `RowMajor`, but the construction operand still needs row-major handling even when the member type stayed `mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e`.\n\n## 6. Private Constant With Row-Major Square Member\n\nGiven a private struct variable initialized from a constant row-major square matrix and later read as a whole matrix.\n\nExpected:\n\n```wgsl\nvar\u003cprivate\u003e s : S_1_1 \u003d S_1_1(mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e(\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(0.0f, 4.0f, 8.0f, 12.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 5.0f, 9.0f, 13.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(2.0f, 6.0f, 10.0f, 14.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(3.0f, 7.0f, 11.0f, 15.0f)));\n\nv \u003d (vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f) * transpose(s.m));\n```\n\nGot before the patch:\n\n```wgsl\nvar\u003cprivate\u003e s : S_1_1 \u003d S_1_1(mat4x4\u003cf32\u003e(\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(0.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(4.0f, 5.0f, 6.0f, 7.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(8.0f, 9.0f, 10.0f, 11.0f),\n    vec4\u003cf32\u003e(12.0f, 13.0f, 14.0f, 15.0f)));\n\nv \u003d (vec4\u003cf32\u003e(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f, 4.0f) * s.m);\n```\n\nThis shows the same issue for constants: the constant payload must be rewritten using the original row-major member metadata, and later reads still need the semantic transpose.\n\n## Control Case\n\nThe repro suite also includes a rectangular row-major `mat4x3` whole-load control. It already emitted `transpose(u.m)` before the patch and still does after the patch. That supports the intended scope: the existing non-square path was mostly working because the type changed, while square matrices needed explicit row-major provenance tracking.",
      "revId": "4980e21bb32ae66e5b91e95117b467fa065eb411",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
